<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swathi Thirunal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1813–1846</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Swathi Thirunal Rama Varma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Swathi Thirunal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> Varma (1813–1846) was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Travancore from 1829 to 1846. A patron of learning and the arts, he founded an observatory, a public library and an English school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a>, and composed hundreds of works of Carnatic music. His later years were overshadowed by growing interference from the British Resident.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swathi-thirunal.jpg" alt="Swathi Thirunal" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stephen Crening · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swathi_Thirunal_of_Travancore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
